{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"fattish-plucky-bufflehead","creation_channel":"mcp","description":"## Context / Problem\n\nAgents creating tickets are not shown similar existing tickets, so duplicate bugs and epics accumulate and fragment work (operator ruling 2026-09-06). The first implementation of this capability, epic only-crave-art (bd83-c56f-1f72-4ef2, ADR 0086, landed 2026-07-09/10), was switched off on 2026-08-27 (`[llm] overlap_drain = \"off\"`, ticket eyeable-intimate-marmoset 62ab) and 2026-08-31 (`verify.suggest_duplicate_tickets = false`, ticket horrendous-limpid-chamois 4eae) because its persistence and coordination design overloaded the shared tickets branch. Its code is removed by epic bossy-skittish-foal (cea1-c784-99e0-446a); this epic is the design record for the replacement and the container for its research, design decision, and implementation.\n\n## What the first implementation did (architecture as built; paths are at the commit before its removal, recorded on the closing comment of the removal story under epic bossy-skittish-foal)\n\n- Enrichment: an LLM-extracted \"Cupid digest\" per ticket (`problem_keywords[]`, `component_or_area`, `key_entities[]`, `propositions[2..6]`; schema `src/rebar/schemas/ticket_digest.schema.json`, prompt `src/rebar/llm/reviewers/ticket_digest.md`, trivial model class) from title + description + every comment.\n- Storage: the digest persisted as a signed `TICKET_DIGEST` event (~2.8 KB) in the ticket directory on the shared, auto-pushed `tickets` branch (`src/rebar/llm/overlap/digest_sidecar.py`). Queue coordination also persisted as events (`ENQUEUE_ENRICH`/`CLAIM_ENRICH`/`DONE_ENRICH`, `src/rebar/llm/overlap/queue.py`), 3 to 4 pushed commits per enrichment.\n- Trigger: a drain gate on every store write (`src/rebar/_store/event_append.py`, `src/rebar/_store/push.py` -> `enrich_drain.maybe_drain`) spawning a detached interpreter with leases and advisory locks.\n- Retrieval: brute-force BM25F over all fresh digests, rebuilt per query from a full reduce of every ticket (`src/rebar/llm/overlap/retrieve.py`; ~48 s per query on 8,938 ticket dirs, measured 2026-09-06), graph exclusion (`graph.py`), top-20.\n- Judge: pairwise LLM judge over batches of 6 candidates in both orderings, both orderings must agree at confidence >= 0.7 and cite a shared artifact, at most 3 findings surfaced as ready-to-run `rebar link` commands, never auto-linked (`judge.py`; calibration `scripts/overlap_judge_calibrate.py`, `docs/calibration/overlap_batch_confidence.md`).\n- Surfacing: only after a plan-review certification (`plan_review/attest.py` enqueue; `plan_review/__init__.py` `verdict[\"overlap\"]`). Nothing ran at `create`. The only create-time check was a same-normalized-title probe over a 600 s journal (`src/rebar/_commands/recent_creates.py`), which stays.\n\n## Why it failed\nSix bug clusters, all traced to the storage and trigger design (prune outside the lock; prune breaking union-merge; unbounded growth and write storms; a write-path gate that is O(store); an unbounded, mis-contracted judge; detached-child hygiene) plus an un-ticketed cross-environment ping-pong on the freshness key. The cluster-by-cluster ticket list and the inventory of reusable pieces are in this epic's first comment.\n\n## Measured facts for the redesign\n\n- Store: 4,285 tickets on the hot path snapshot, 8,960 ticket directories on the live tracker, 147k event files.\n- Duplicates are semantic: of 51 known duplicate pairs in the store, 44 are semantic rather than lexical; exact-title recall was 14% (7/51) (ticket exemplary-hexahedral-needlefish eac3, 2026-08-21).\n- Paraphrase retrieval: digest-BM25F retrieved 8 of 8 heavy paraphrases at rank 1 (recall@20 = 1.0), stable from a 60- to a 200-ticket corpus, where raw-text BM25 degraded 0.875 -> 0.75 (only-crave-art derisking experiment, N = 8).\n- Tickets are large: enrichment prompts reached 206k tokens before bounding (spongy-illjudged-terrier 569c), so any semantic method needs summarization before embedding.\n- Embeddings were rejected in July on scale grounds (\"ANN pays off only >100K vectors\") with N = 8; that decision is reopened by this epic.\n\n## Operator framing (2026-09-06)\n\nThis is a vector search problem over summaries of large tickets. A lexical-only local index is insufficient and provably so against the historical corpus.\n\n## Constraints every design must satisfy (from the OSS survey of git-bug, Fossil, Jujutsu, Cargo, Zoekt, Gitea, Zulip, Discourse, Bugzilla, GitLab, GitHub, Linear, Sentry)\n\n- Derived search data never lives as records on the `tickets` branch; it is a disposable, version-keyed index outside the replicated store (git-bug keeps Bleve under `.git/git-bug/`; Fossil rebuilds FTS from artifacts; Cargo keys its cache by commit SHA).\n- No indexing work is spawned from the write path; catch-up happens in the reader's process on next use, keyed by the tickets-branch HEAD SHA, with full rebuild on any mismatch.\n- Compose-time suggestion is non-blocking, capped (3 to 5 results), and gated on a minimum title length (Discourse, GitHub, Bugzilla).\n- Model calls are bounded and off the write path: summarization and embedding run in a batch step; an LLM judge runs only on demand over at most 10 lexical-or-vector candidates (Sentry embeds only on a fingerprint miss; Discourse AI and Linear embed in background jobs).\n- Correctness never depends on a daemon; the optional MCP server may pre-warm on a timer using the same code path.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] An ADR records the chosen retrieval architecture (summarization method, embedding model and store, index location and freshness key, catch-up strategy) with measured recall@5 and recall@10 on the 51-pair duplicate benchmark built from the store, and the measurements are reproducible from a committed benchmark spec. Checked by: the ADR exists under `docs/adr/` with the numbers and the benchmark command.\n- [ ] `rebar create` returns the top-5 similar existing tickets (id, alias, title, score) as an advisory field on all three surfaces (CLI `--output json`, library return dict, MCP `create_ticket` output) within a p95 of 500 ms on a 5,000-ticket store when the index is warm. Checked by: the interface parity tests plus a timing test over a synthetic 5,000-ticket store.\n- [ ] No derived search data is written to the `tickets` branch: after 100 creates and 100 reads on a test store, the tickets branch contains only user events. Checked by: an integration test that asserts the event-type set of the branch after the run.\n- [ ] A newly created ticket is findable by the next `create` on the same clone without any background process running. Checked by: an integration test with no MCP server and no CI.\n- [ ] Plan review surfaces overlap findings against the same index on demand with a bounded judge budget (at most 10 candidates, at most 2 LLM calls). Checked by: the plan-review sidecar test asserting the call count.\n\n## Child decomposition\n\n- Research and design (first child, created now): builds the 51-pair benchmark, measures the candidate architectures, writes the ADR. Delivers criterion 1.\n- Index and create-time surfacing: delivers criteria 2, 3, 4.\n- Plan-review overlap on demand: delivers criterion 5.\nChildren after the first are created when the ADR lands.","id":"62dc-535b-5d05-421d","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":["complexity-reduction","design-record"],"ticket_type":"epic","title":"Similar-ticket surfacing v2: design record, research, and vector search over ticket summaries"},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1788804880723295410,"uuid":"b3dedc3a-b3ca-4b03-81cf-5a69708f0a28"}